{
  "dom_id": "step-36348",
  "name": "Example: \n                    Identifying the Type and Strength of a Given Correlation",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14226558/assets/aa9f4ce130811186b476064a3d0b828daf9f5ded3d9011eda832f3bc470f8334.png)\n\nThe scatterplot above shows the correspondence between the mean daily temperature outside a particular coffee shop and the number of customers ordering hot drinks. Which of the following best describes the correlation between the variables in the scatter plot?",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14226558/assets/b970e539af5b695644486ad4eb7c21558c334ffd33c1df1a2343fac493d84389.png)\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The points in the scatter plot seem to follow a straight line with a *negative* slope. So, we have a *negative* correlation.\n\n- The points are scattered quite far from the corresponding trend line. So, we have a *weak* correlation.\n\nTherefore, the variables have a *weak negative* linear correlation.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
